From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
Subject: [PATCH] usb: typec: rts5453: ack the shared IRQ even when ARA is silent

STATUS: NOT BUILT, NOT BOOT-TESTED. Authored from a live diagnosis of
MS-R1 ("TinyTerror", kernel 7.1.5-cix, BIOS 1.0) on 2026-10-10. Applies
after 0076 and 0078. Hunk line numbers are derived from the post-0078
file; apply with `patch -p1 -F3` or adjust context if needed.

Problem (measured on hardware, not inferred)
--------------------------------------------
MS-R1 wires two RTS5453 PD controllers (i2c-3 addresses 0x30 and 0x31,
ACPI CIXH200D:00/:01) to one active-low GpioInt, which lands on Linux
IRQ 144 (trigger type 0x8 = IRQ_TYPE_LEVEL_LOW).

0078 hands that IRQ to the generic SMBus ARA core
(i2c_new_smbus_alert_device(); smbus_alert() in drivers/i2c/i2c-smbus.c)
and never requests the IRQ itself. On this board no chip answers an ARA
read at 0x0C, so smbus_alert() takes its `if (status < 0) break;` path,
falls through, and returns IRQ_HANDLED *unconditionally* while the line
is still asserted. Because the handler claims the IRQ every time, the
kernel's "nobody cared" spurious-IRQ backstop can never engage, so
nothing breaks the loop.

Live evidence:
  /proc/interrupts  144: <1.15e9>  cdns-gpio  7 Level  smbus_alert
                    46,674 interrupts in 6 s (~7,800/s) at 14.7 d uptime
  irq/144-smbus_a   ~6.2% CPU continuously
  dmesg             zero "nobody cared" occurrences
  debugfs (0078)    members=2, ara=registered, alerts=0 on BOTH members,
                    status_reads=1, irq_acks=0, offline_events=0
  gpio-7            "PD01 GpioInt(0) in lo IRQ ACTIVE LOW" - held low

`alerts` is incremented immediately after the protocol/removing/offline
guards in rts5453_alert(), and both members report online=1 faulted=0, so
alerts=0 proves the ARA dispatch never reached this driver at all. The
driver has therefore never written I2C_INT_ACK_BIT: irq_acks=0. That
missing ack is why the line is never released. 0078 documents the hole
itself: "the generic ARA core owns IRQ request and dispatch entirely
inside i2c-smbus.c; this driver has no hook into its per-IRQ ... counts".

Consequence beyond the wasted CPU: no alert ever arrives, so the Type-C
PD side is frozen at its probe-time state. Both ports reported
[source] [host] with a partner registered once at probe; any post-boot
cable event, role swap, alt-mode/DP negotiation or PD renegotiation is
invisible to the driver.

What this does
--------------
Keeps ARA as the primary dispatch path and adds the missing hook, so the
driver can act when ARA is silent:

  1. The group requests the same physical IRQ for itself with flags
     matching the ARA client's action exactly (IRQF_SHARED | IRQF_ONESHOT
     plus the already-established hardware trigger type). The trigger bits
     are required: __setup_irq() rejects any action after the first whose
     trigger bits differ from irqd_get_trigger_type()
     (kernel/irq/manage.c:1620-1629). With CONFIG_I2C_SMBUS=y the ARA
     client probes synchronously inside i2c_new_smbus_alert_device(), so
     the hardware trigger type is already set when this handler registers.
  2. Every interrupt acks each online member through the existing
     rts5453_capture_and_clear_alert(), which writes I2C_INT_ACK_BIT via
     rts5453_capture_status_locked(rts, true) and so deasserts SMBALERT#
     and ends the storm. Where ARA does work the callback runs too; the
     extra status read is cheap and the apply path is already
     change-guarded.
  3. If the line still will not clear after RTS5453_STORM_NO_PROGRESS_MAX
     rounds the handler masks the IRQ (disable_irq_nosync() - a blocking
     disable_irq() would deadlock inside the threaded handler) and
     schedules a re-arm, so a transient condition recovers instead of
     permanently killing PD. Counters are exposed in debugfs.

Registration is best-effort: if it fails the code warns once and carries
on with ARA-only behaviour, i.e. it degrades to today's behaviour rather
than failing group start.

Future option (deliberately not taken here)
-------------------------------------------
The alternative is to stop sharing the line through the ARA core and go
back to per-client IRQF_SHARED handlers that read each chip's status and
control registers directly - the approach used by the sibling srcshelton
overlay (7.2.x/0026), and ancestral to the vendor
rts5453h_irq_rts_handle() election that 0078 replaced. That drops the ARA
dependency entirely but gives up the "only the alerting chip is serviced"
property, which is why 0078 moved away from it. Kept as a documented
fallback, not the primary path.

diff --git a/drivers/usb/typec/rts5453.c b/drivers/usb/typec/rts5453.c
index e5de1fb..6397eaf 100644
--- a/drivers/usb/typec/rts5453.c
+++ b/drivers/usb/typec/rts5453.c
@@ -129,6 +129,20 @@ struct rts5453_irq_group {
 	unsigned int wake_depth;
 
 	struct dentry *debugfs_dir;
+
+	/*
+	 * ARA is the primary dispatch path, but it cannot report "line
+	 * asserted and no chip answered". This guard handler supplies the
+	 * missing ack so the shared line is actually released; see the
+	 * comment on rts5453_group_irq_handler().
+	 */
+	bool guard_irq;
+	bool storm_masked;
+	unsigned int no_progress;
+	unsigned long storm_last;
+	atomic64_t direct_services;
+	atomic64_t storm_breaks;
+	struct delayed_work storm_rearm_work;
 };
 
 static LIST_HEAD(rts5453_irq_groups);
@@ -921,6 +935,104 @@ static void rts5453_group_irq_wake_disable(struct rts5453_irq_group *group)
 	mutex_unlock(&group->pm_lock);
 }
 
+/*
+ * Bounded storm containment for the shared PD interrupt. Eight consecutive
+ * rounds in which servicing the members did not release the line is treated
+ * as a stuck source: the IRQ is masked and re-armed later rather than left
+ * to spin forever. On MS-R1 it otherwise spins at ~7.8 kHz indefinitely,
+ * because smbus_alert() always reports IRQ_HANDLED and so defeats the
+ * kernel's spurious-interrupt handling.
+ */
+#define RTS5453_STORM_NO_PROGRESS_MAX	8
+#define RTS5453_STORM_REARM_MS		5000
+/* Interrupts closer together than this are treated as one burst. */
+#define RTS5453_STORM_WINDOW_MS		100
+
+/*
+ * Ack every online member of the group. rts5453_capture_and_clear_alert()
+ * writes I2C_INT_ACK_BIT (and HPD_IRQ_ACK when set), which is what actually
+ * deasserts the chip's interrupt output; without it a level-triggered shared
+ * line can never be released.
+ */
+static void rts5453_group_service_members(struct rts5453_irq_group *group)
+{
+	struct rts5453h *rts;
+
+	mutex_lock(&group->members_lock);
+	list_for_each_entry(rts, &group->members, irq_node) {
+		if (READ_ONCE(rts->removing) || READ_ONCE(rts->offline))
+			continue;
+
+		if (rts5453_capture_and_clear_alert(rts) == 0) {
+			atomic64_inc(&group->direct_services);
+			schedule_delayed_work(&rts->apply_work, 0);
+		}
+	}
+	mutex_unlock(&group->members_lock);
+}
+
+static void rts5453_group_storm_rearm(struct work_struct *work)
+{
+	struct rts5453_irq_group *group =
+		container_of(to_delayed_work(work), struct rts5453_irq_group,
+			     storm_rearm_work);
+
+	group->no_progress = 0;
+	group->storm_masked = false;
+	enable_irq(group->irq);
+}
+
+/*
+ * Safety-net handler for the ARA-owned line. 0078 hands the physical IRQ to
+ * the generic SMBus ARA core, which cannot tell the driver that the line is
+ * asserted while no chip answers the ARA read - smbus_alert() simply returns
+ * IRQ_HANDLED and the level-triggered line re-fires forever. This handler is
+ * registered in addition to the ARA client, with the same flags and trigger
+ * type, and supplies the missing ack; when ARA does dispatch, its callback
+ * runs as before and this handler's extra status read is harmless because
+ * the apply path is already change-guarded.
+ */
+static irqreturn_t rts5453_group_irq_handler(int irq, void *data)
+{
+	struct rts5453_irq_group *group = data;
+
+	rts5453_group_service_members(group);
+
+	/*
+	 * Only interrupts arriving in a tight burst count as storm evidence:
+	 * a genuinely stuck level-triggered line fires thousands of times per
+	 * second, whereas normal PD events are widely spaced. Resetting on the
+	 * gap keeps this a "consecutive" detector; without it the counter was
+	 * cumulative and every 8th interrupt masked the shared IRQ for
+	 * RTS5453_STORM_REARM_MS, silently dropping Type-C/PD events.
+	 */
+	if (time_after(jiffies,
+		       group->storm_last + msecs_to_jiffies(RTS5453_STORM_WINDOW_MS)))
+		group->no_progress = 0;
+	group->storm_last = jiffies;
+
+	if (++group->no_progress < RTS5453_STORM_NO_PROGRESS_MAX)
+		return IRQ_HANDLED;
+
+	/*
+	 * Still asserted after repeatedly acking every member. Mask the line
+	 * and retry later. disable_irq_nosync() is required: the blocking
+	 * disable_irq() waits for this very handler to finish.
+	 */
+	group->no_progress = 0;
+	group->storm_masked = true;
+	atomic64_inc(&group->storm_breaks);
+	dev_warn_ratelimited(&group->adapter->dev,
+			     "RTS5453 shared IRQ %d did not clear after %u acks; masking and re-arming in %u ms\n",
+			     irq, RTS5453_STORM_NO_PROGRESS_MAX,
+			     RTS5453_STORM_REARM_MS);
+	disable_irq_nosync(irq);
+	schedule_delayed_work(&group->storm_rearm_work,
+			      msecs_to_jiffies(RTS5453_STORM_REARM_MS));
+
+	return IRQ_HANDLED;
+}
+
 static ssize_t disable_pd_interrupt_show(struct device *dev,
 		struct device_attribute *attr,
 		char *buf)
@@ -1041,6 +1153,28 @@ static int rts5453_group_start(struct rts5453_irq_group *group)
 		return ret;
 	}
 
+	/*
+	 * ARA owns the line, but it cannot tell us that the line is asserted
+	 * while nobody answers. Register our own shared handler so members
+	 * still get acked (and the line released) in that case. The trigger
+	 * bits must be the hardware's, not zero: __setup_irq() compares them
+	 * against irqd_get_trigger_type() for every action after the first.
+	 */
+	ret = request_threaded_irq(group->irq, NULL, rts5453_group_irq_handler,
+				   IRQF_SHARED | IRQF_ONESHOT |
+				   group->trigger_type,
+				   "rts5453h-ara-guard", group);
+	if (ret) {
+		dev_warn(&group->adapter->dev,
+			 "RTS5453 shared IRQ %d: guard handler not registered (%d); continuing ARA-only\n",
+			 group->irq, ret);
+	} else {
+		group->guard_irq = true;
+		dev_info(&group->adapter->dev,
+			 "RTS5453 shared IRQ %d: guard handler registered (trigger=0x%x)\n",
+			 group->irq, group->trigger_type);
+	}
+
 	return 0;
 }
 
@@ -1079,6 +1213,11 @@ static int rts5453_group_counters_show(struct seq_file *s, void *unused)
 	seq_printf(s, "irq_disable_depth=%u wake_depth=%u\n",
 		   group->irq_disable_depth, group->wake_depth);
 	mutex_unlock(&group->pm_lock);
+	seq_printf(s, "guard_irq=%u direct_services=%lld storm_breaks=%lld no_progress=%u\n",
+		   (unsigned int)group->guard_irq,
+		   (long long)atomic64_read(&group->direct_services),
+		   (long long)atomic64_read(&group->storm_breaks),
+		   group->no_progress);
 	return 0;
 }
 DEFINE_SHOW_ATTRIBUTE(rts5453_group_counters);
@@ -1157,6 +1296,8 @@ static int rts5453_group_join(struct rts5453h *rts)
 		INIT_LIST_HEAD(&group->members);
 		mutex_init(&group->members_lock);
 		mutex_init(&group->pm_lock);
+		INIT_DELAYED_WORK(&group->storm_rearm_work,
+				  rts5453_group_storm_rearm);
 		refcount_set(&group->refs, 1);
 		group->adapter = client->adapter;
 		group->irq = client->irq;
@@ -1297,11 +1438,35 @@ static void rts5453_group_leave(struct rts5453h *rts)
 	 * IRQ, via free_irq(), which itself blocks until any currently
 	 * running instance of the threaded handler completes. No manual
 	 * disable_irq()/synchronize_irq()/free_irq() sequence is needed
-	 * here; this driver never requests the physical IRQ itself.
+	 * here for the ARA client; the guard handler added below is requested
+	 * separately and is therefore freed explicitly.
 	 */
 	if (group->ara_client)
 		i2c_unregister_device(group->ara_client);
 
+	/*
+	 * The guard handler is ours and is not devres-managed by the ARA
+	 * client, so it must be released explicitly before the group is
+	 * freed. Cancel the re-arm first so it cannot enable_irq() after
+	 * free_irq().
+	 */
+	if (group->guard_irq) {
+		/*
+		 * Release the IRQ FIRST: free_irq() synchronises with any
+		 * running handler, so nothing can re-arm the re-arm work
+		 * afterwards. Cancelling the work first left a window in
+		 * which a handler could re-schedule it after the only
+		 * cancel, and it would then fire on the freed group.
+		 * Re-balance a mask left by the storm breaker first.
+		 */
+		if (group->storm_masked) {
+			group->storm_masked = false;
+			enable_irq(group->irq);
+		}
+		free_irq(group->irq, group);
+	}
+	cancel_delayed_work_sync(&group->storm_rearm_work);
+
 	debugfs_remove_recursive(group->debugfs_dir);
 
 	mutex_destroy(&group->members_lock);
